       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 9,600 mg/day of ibuprofen is about 137 mg/kg/day for a 70 kg patient. That is three times the maximum labeled dose, and I know of no indication that supports it. The extra dose adds no analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit, because the effect plateaus well below it. It does raise the risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function. You didn't give a rationale, so I can't treat this as an intentional protocol.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter the maximum standard dose:** ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily is 3,200 mg/day. Or enter a lower dose, such as 600 mg PO four times daily.\n- **Help you with other options:** If pain control is the goal, I can enter scheduled acetaminophen or another adjunct alongside ibuprofen.\n- **Route the order through pharmacy:** If you have a specific clinical reason, such as a research protocol or a consult recommendation, I can enter it with a note once pharmacy or a toxicology/clinical pharmacology consult has reviewed it. I'd need you to tell me what that reason is.\n\nTell me which of these you want, and I'll enter it right away."
